.TH UPASFS 4
.SH NAME
upas/fs \- present a mailbox as a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B upas/fs
[
.B -m
.I mountpt
]
.I mbox
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Upas/fs
reads the Unix mbox file
.I mbox
and serves it as a file system, the Plan 9
.I upas
way, mounted at
.I mountpt
(default
.BR /mail/fs/mbox ).
Each message becomes a numbered directory
.RB ( 1 ,
.BR 2 ,
\&...) so any program can read mail with
.IR ls (1)
and
.IR cat (1)
rather than parsing message syntax itself.
.PP
Within a message directory the read-only files are:
.TP
.B raw
the whole message as stored.
.TP
.B header
the header block (up to the first blank line).
.TP
.B body
the message body.
.TP
.B unixheader
the mbox
.B From_
envelope line.
.TP
.BR from ", " to ", " subject ", " date ", " cc ", " reply-to ", " message-id
the value of the corresponding header field, with continuation lines
folded; an absent field reads as empty.
.PP
The server keeps running after it prints its start line; the mount lives
in the invoking name space.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX

	upas/fs -m /mail/fs/mbox $home/mbox
	ls /mail/fs/mbox
	cat /mail/fs/mbox/1/subject

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/upas/fs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR upas-fetch (1),
.IR imap4 (2)
.SH BUGS
Only a local Unix mbox is read; feeding the same presentation from
.IR imap4 (2)
(so a Gmail inbox is browsable this way) is not yet wired in.
Only the common header fields are broken out, and MIME parts are not
decoded \(em
.B body
is the raw body.
The mailbox is read once at start; it is not reread as it changes.
